cartridge "FIDGITS MUSIC SCHOOL/WALTER E. MEYERS/1986"

' FIDGITS MUSIC SCHOOL
' A ColecoVision game based on "Fidgits Music School" by Walter E. Meyers,
' Ahoy! July 1986 (Commodore 64 BASIC with custom characters and sprites;
' disk ahoy1986-07, 38 blocks). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu. The fidgits are the square
' baby birds of games/fidgits (the same author's FIDGITS, Ahoy! August 1985).
'
' The original (orig/fmusic.bas): pick the G clef or the F clef. Seven
' times, a hidden word made of the letters A-G (51 words: BAGGAGE, FACADE,
' CABBAGE...) is written as whole notes on a staff, one note at a time,
' with a fidgit under each note; each note sounds as it appears (treble
' C4-B4, bass C3-B3, middle C on a ledger line). Then, note by note, press
' the key with the note's name: a wrong key makes the fidgit squawk and
' waits for another key; the right one names the fidgit (it takes its
' colour and letter), sounds the note and writes the letter over the
' staff. When the word is spelt the fidgits "show off": each sings its
' note with two more (a chord). A word is not repeated until 40 have been
' used. After seven words, a nest picture asks to play again or quit.
' No score, no time limit.
'
' This version: the same seven hidden words from the same 51, the same two
' clefs and note ranges, the squawk, the colour and letter for a named
' fidgit, the show-off chords, no word repeated before 40 are used.
'
' Liberties: joystick only - a piano keyboard C D E F G A B lies under the
' branch and the mother bird of FIDGITS perches over the chosen key; the
' stick moves her, a button presses the key (the C64 game used the letter
' keys). To make it a scored game: each note has a patience bar; the
' points for a note are 40 x the patience left (0-40), a wrong key costs
' 120 frames of patience, a note left until the bar runs out is named for
' you (shown, sung, no points). A word spelt with no mistake earns 50. The
' patience shrinks word by word (8.7 s to 4.7 s). A high score is kept.
' The note to name is red and its fidgit sings and repeats its note every
' two seconds (the original showed all the notes black and played each
' once as it was drawn). The notes appear all at once (the original drew
' them one by one with their sounds). The letters appear under the
' fidgits on the branch, and the whole word is printed after the show-off.
' Show-off chords are the diatonic triad (root, third, fifth) on the three
' tone channels (the listing added the fourth and sixth scale steps). The
' title's random-note song is replaced by an original tune played once;
' the nest end screen by a message (house rules). Short words are centred.
'
' Controls: either controller; stick left/right moves the mother bird over
' the keys (hold to repeat); either button plays the key. On the title,
' up/down choose, a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table; the title tune
' is played once. `set sprite count` is always given. Own xorshift random
' numbers. Nothing flashes.

